TEXTS = {
 "r15-sit": "And then there’s the situationship, which is hard to define because that’s basically the point. Usually it’s a happenstance relationship based on sex. There’s less communication, and what communication exists is more superficial. Yet it is obviously some kind of relationship even while one or both people refuse to name it. You’re regularly having sex, going out together, spending time alone together, and doing things you wouldn’t do with a regular friend, so it feels like it’s filling a relationship void, but only accidentally and occasionally—maybe just enough to keep you from looking for anything deeper.\n\nIt can go either way, but often the man wants to keep it undefined while the woman wants commitment, partly because she’s the one risking pregnancy. Whoever keeps it undefined gets the fun without the responsibility. People still get pregnant and attached, though, whether or not anybody named the relationship, and the one keeping it undefined still ends up with the existential void another superficial fix creates. Doomscrolling entertains you too.\n\nAnd “Why are you making this heavy?” can be a real question. They may actually mean: don’t you want the fun, and are you about to give it up? My answer would be: I’m trying to understand whether this is really fulfilling me or if it’s drainingly superficial.\n\nI had something sort of like that with H., although ours was much deeper than what people normally mean by situationship. We were (and still are) BFFs, and spent nearly all our time together. She was falling in love with me, and I wasn’t falling in love with her. She felt more like a sister to me, partly because I’m not attracted to white women. Ours wasn’t superficial, just painfully uneven.",
 "r15-slow": "Going slowly can show you how somebody’s moods move over time, how they treat you, and how they treat other people, but it still can’t tell you how good the sex will be or how either of you will act afterward.\n\nYou can know somebody for twenty years and then discover on the first night that the polarity isn’t there. Touch or smell can feel wrong, or your desire levels or kinks don’t fit. Maybe one person is sexually shut down, or the bodies just don’t connect.\n\nSome of those things might get better if both partners are committed to helping each other through it. Sometimes bad sexual fit still feels like, “Let’s just be friends!”\n\nSaying, “The sex isn’t working for me,” may sound like, “I only love you if the sex is good.” But universal love doesn’t make someone special. It isn’t romance. It’s the basis of any good relationship of any kind.\n\nI can still love her and not want to be in a romantic relationship with her. Romantic love generally needs good sex too, or we’re friends who care deeply about each other.",
 "r15-disc": "Self-reflection is a good place to start, but it can’t see what it can’t see.\n\nA peer can give another angle. Your partner is also usually trying to keep you, so they have an ulterior motive that a peer counselor hopefully does not.\n\nI recommend peer counseling outside the couple: if my partner is the only person I bring all my pain to, she also ends up carrying whatever I do when I still don’t understand it.\n\nI also have to deal with what happens in my body: my mind may understand that my partner is going through something difficult and that I don’t have to be dragged down with her, but my body still feels dragged down, fragile, and sensitive. My breathing gets shallow, and it becomes extremely hard to feel okay while she is in pain.\n\nCrying helps a lot, but it may actually be harder for men once they are in a relationship because they are expected to be the shoulder everybody else cries on. That is another reason to have a peer counselor.\n\nCrying also contains a type of automatic erratic breathwork. Breathwork is one of the easiest ways I know to calm down. Reduced breathing or the Buteyko method is especially useful to me when my partner is getting me upset and flustered with drama. Sometimes she’s deliberately trying to do that, and other times it’s hard to tell. I explain more of the body-based options in my Somatic Modalities guide.\n\nMeditation is also helpful, but for most people, I would still put inner-child reparenting, peer counseling, and body-based work first or make them primary meditation targets. Many people say they cannot meditate or have not gotten to that stage yet, and meditation without grounding in those approaches can easily be a road into spiritual bypassing.",
 "r15-jeal367": "If only the serious attractions are supposed to be disclosed, each disclosure becomes such a major event that people are less likely to say anything at all, so I would rather make every attraction possible to mention casually, including the low-stakes ones.\n\nPeople promise a foundational, permanent, exclusive romantic partnership far more often than they actually live one. In a polyamorous relationship the boundaries might be more negotiable, but that actually means negotiating is required.",
 "r15-jeal369": "Polyamory isn’t automatically better, though, because it can be dishonest too. These days, I believe it is often a way to cope with an inability to connect deeply with one person, or a way to admit a lack of self-control without necessarily doing the self-development that would make the arrangement ethical. Sometimes pretending to be monogamous does more damage than an outside connection would have, so if you can’t do monogamy, it’s still more honest to say so.\n\nI learned most of this by screwing it up, and some of it I just thought through, so I’m still not sure about all of it.",
 "r15-deeper": "And the question is more than just a practical one. It’s philosophical. Men have this (often subconscious) insecurity inside the provider role: how much does she love me, and how much does she love what I can do for her?\n\nI don’t need the answer to be that what I provide has nothing to do with it. Maybe she likes that I’m competent, protective, useful, and can make things happen. I definitely like her beauty and her sexuality. I just want what we have to grow bigger than whatever first made us want each other. Because as Bee always said, “You’re either going up, or going down.”\n\nI had wondered for years how much a woman would love me if I couldn’t really provide for her. Then Bee told me, “I would rather be with you in the forest than with any other man in a mansion.” That’s what made me fall in love with her.\n\nI got a smaller and much less romantic test later. Bee gave me some shoes to try that didn’t fit. I wore them for a day, and somehow my knee ended up messed up for about a month. She did nurse me, but a bit reluctantly. She also kept suggesting it was partly in my head, or not really that bad. I get why, since neither of us could believe one day in the wrong shoes could do that. It still felt dismissive, though, like part of her just wanted my knee to be fine.\n\nBefore me, Bee had also stayed with a boyfriend who was totally broke and crazy and supported him for a while. Later she blamed herself for staying through that, and I agree: helping carry somebody through temporary incapacity is different from becoming the permanent support system for a man who won’t take responsibility. Some women are fixer types, which can create the opposite pattern.\n\nWhat makes me desirable is not the same question as what makes me loved when I temporarily stop being desirable in that particular way.",
 "r15-ideal": "Idealization can be pathological, but it can also come very close to real deep love and appreciation. That’s why it’s so easy to get confused.\n\nSo far in my life the main experiences of this have been with Bee and Key. They’re also the two women I’ve actually been in love with, which is probably not a coincidence. Both of them saw me as their knight in shining armor, and that brought out this divine-feminine beauty in them that made me into a real love slave. I’m not Superman out here trying to “save a hoe.” There’s just a natural director/muse dynamic that sometimes plays out in an exaggerated way.\n\nIt shouldn’t have to happen that way, though. It’s a bad shortcut to the divine archetypes.\n\nLegitimately seeing someone as amazing doesn’t depend on what the seeing gives you. You’re already lost in the sauce once you start identifying with the way you see them, like she’s perfect, this is destiny, I’m the one who finally found her.\n\nThe degree of idealization also predicts the degree of demonization for me. I don’t mean this as a clinical diagnosis, but I use it almost diagnostically in my own life. If a woman says, “You’re such a great guy. Of course you have flaws like anyone, but I love you,” that sounds healthy. If she says, “You’re perfect. I can’t find any flaw,” I’m already running.\n\nWhen the BPD splitting happens, private hatred can replace worship, and then come public accusations, rewriting the whole past and treating every flaw as proof that the person was fake all along. Sometimes the woman who thought I was perfect later accuses me of thinking I’m perfect.\n\nI’ve learned to get nervous when somebody knows too much about me too soon. We’re twin flames. She knows my motives. I’m Lord Krishna. Okay, maybe I really am way better than the other guys she’s found. But if she hasn’t found anything about me that bothers her yet, she hasn’t found all of me.\n\nFor example, I still love women I’ve loved before. Sometimes I talk about them because I’m thinking about them. I’m not trying to make her jealous. If she can’t handle that I still love them, she isn’t ready for me.\n\nIf she says, “Can you talk less about your exes?” Sure. Maybe I do talk about them too much sometimes. But they still matter to me. If I’m supposed to listen to everything on her mind, I don’t want to have to pretend that isn’t on mine."
}
